ABS_TXT=(f"About 40 million people in the United States drink from unmonitored private wells, and health departments must choose which to test first. Machine-learning risk maps are the usual guide but are judged with random splits. We simulated testing campaigns on 53,294 arsenic, 28,117 uranium, 150,299 fluoride and 47,375 nitrate wells from USGS records, holding out one state at a time. A random split overstated map skill (arsenic ROC-AUC {f1(rv.loc['random_5fold','auc_roc'])} versus {f1(rv.loc['leave_state_out','auc_roc'])}). With 20% of a new state's wells tested, an arsenic map from other states found {f1(a10('M1_map'))} times the unsafe wells that random order would, no better than chance, whereas testing next to wells that had just returned unsafe results found {f1(a10('M4_feedback'))} times as many and cut tests per unsafe well from {t_rand:.1f} to {t_fb:.1f}.{REPLAY_ABS} For fluoride a transferred map worked ({f1(f2('M1_map'))}), so the best strategy depends on the contaminant. Pure hot-spot chasing under-served high-poverty counties, and splitting each batch by county poverty removed this at almost no cost. Gains shrank when results returned late; wells are not a random sample and no field trial was done.")
P("Abstract: "+ABS_TXT,count=False)
H1("Introduction")
P(f"About 40 million people in the United States use private wells that fall outside public-supply regulation ({C('Methods and Uncertainty in Predictions')}). Owners or health agencies must decide whether to test them ({C('Reducing public exposure')}); data-driven support for private-well decisions has appeared in this journal ({C('Responding after Hurricane Helene')}). Agencies run outreach campaigns with limited budgets and must decide where to start, for example by ranking towns on well use and past water quality ({C('Private well ranking indices')}). Machine learning is widely applied to groundwater quality ({C('Application of machine learning in groundwater quality')}), and maps estimate the chance that a private well exceeds an arsenic threshold ({C('Machine-Learning Predictions of High Arsenic')}; {C('Machine Learning Models of Arsenic in Private Wells','Surface Variable')}). Unregulated wells in Nevada and Iowa show how widespread such metals are ({C('Naturally occurring metals in unregulated')}; {C('Iowa Population Exposures')}), and early-warning models exist for lead in private systems ({C('early warning systems to predict water lead')}). The national model of {C('Machine Learning Models of Arsenic in Private Wells')} reported 33.9% sensitivity for exceedances of 10 µg/L on test wells drawn as a stratified 30% subset of all wells, with no spatial hold-out. Random splits place neighbouring wells in both training and test sets, and buffered spatial validation gives lower scores ({C('Machine learning for predicting groundwater quality')}; {C('National-scale evaluation of groundwater fluoride')}). Fluoride models moved between basins only with hydrochemical predictors ({C('Transferability of Machine Learning Models')}).")
P(f"Maps of arsenic and related contaminants are well developed at global, national and local scales ({C('Global threat of arsenic','Revealing the sources of arsenic','Anthropogenic and Geogenic')}; {C('high-fluoride groundwater in China','Machine learning-based prediction of elevated uranium')}). Agencies already target outreach by past results, for example by retesting owners whose earlier results lay between an old and a new standard ({C('Targeted Private Well Outreach')}) or by holding testing events for at-risk wells ({C('Evaluating the impact of free private well testing')}). Spatially structured validation is studied in ecology and mapping ({C('Cross‐validation strategies','Spatial validation reveals','Predicting into unknown space','block CV')}), though how best to judge map accuracy is debated ({C('Spatial cross-validation is not the right way')}).")
P(f"A campaign is also not a one-shot prediction. Results arrive as testing proceeds, and neighbouring wells tend to share geology. Letters citing a neighbour's exceedance made owners more likely to test ({C('Reducing public exposure')}), and the idea of following hits into their neighbourhood is classical in adaptive cluster sampling ({C('Adaptive Cluster Sampling')}). Active search for rare targets is mature ({C('Bayesian Optimal Active Search','Active pointillistic pattern search','Containing a spread through sequential learning')}), including for geospatial discovery ({C('Adapting Actively on the Fly')}). Lead-pipe programmes already combine model predictions with sequential active learning and have backtested it against the order actually used in Flint ({C('ActiveRemediation')}; {C('Flint Water Crisis')}). We therefore do not present feedback as a new idea. What is missing is a measurement, on national data held out by state, of how much a risk map from other states, feedback from tested wells and a state's own history are each worth in tests saved. We provide it for arsenic, uranium, fluoride and nitrate, and we test a rule that decides online how far to trust a map.")
H1("Methods")
P(f"We downloaded discrete well-water results from the USGS Samples API ({C('USGS Samples Data API')}) and kept the earliest valid result per well: arsenic (53,294 wells; 10.0% above 10 µg/L), uranium (28,117 wells; 5.1% above 30 µg/L) and fluoride (150,299 wells; 2.3% above 4 mg/L), with additional lower thresholds. Wells with identical coordinates were collapsed. Predictors known before testing were latitude, longitude and aquifer code; we added lithology and age from the USGS geologic map compilation ({C('State Geologic Map Compilation')}) and NASA POWER climate and elevation ({C('Prediction Of Worldwide Energy')}). A planner holds only these predictors.")
P(f"Each state with at least 100 wells and 15 unsafe wells was held out in turn. All wells within 25 km of the held-out wells were removed from training, and eight states drawn at random before any model was run served only for tuning. In a simulated campaign, wells are tested in batches of 10; a method sees the outcomes of tested wells before choosing the next batch. The score is unsafe wells found when 20% of wells are tested, divided by the number expected under random order (enrichment; random = 1). Inference used paired Wilcoxon tests ({C('Individual Comparisons by Ranking Methods')}) across states, bootstrap intervals ({C('Bootstrap Methods')}) and Holm correction.")
P(f"Methods were: (i) a static gradient-boosting map trained on other states; (ii) kernel feedback, which ranks untested wells by a Gaussian-weighted share of unsafe results among tested neighbours; (iii) a rule that only follows unsafe results; (iv) a hybrid that uses the map as a prior inside the kernel rule; and (v) the adaptive Bayesian-mixture feedback (ABF) rule proposed here. ABF holds 31 experts that differ in spatial scale (2-50 km) and in how much they trust the map (none, partial, full). It weights each expert by exp(−cumulative log loss on wells already tested) and ranks wells by the weighted prediction. Its cumulative prediction loss provably exceeds the best expert's by at most ln 31 nats (a bound on prediction, not discovery). ABF has no tuned parameter. All choices were fixed before evaluation or made on the tuning states.")
H1("Results and Discussion")
P(f"Random splits overstate maps. For arsenic, the same model reached ROC-AUC {f1(rv.loc['random_5fold','auc_roc'])} under a random split and {f1(rv.loc['leave_state_out','auc_roc'])} with held-out states (both pooled over states; the mean within-state AUC was {WSAUC:.2f}, so within a new state a map ranks wells barely better than chance); recall at 20% of wells tested fell from {f1(rv.loc['random_5fold','recall_at_20'])} to {f1(rv.loc['leave_state_out','recall_at_20'])}.")
P(f"For arsenic, a map from other states is close to random order in a new state (Figure 1a, Figure 2a; richer maps in the SI): it is below random order in {int((per.M1_static_lgbm<1).sum())} of {len(per)} states, whereas adaptive testing (Figure 1b) is above it in {int((per.M4_neighbor_only>1).sum())}. It found {f1(a10('M1_map'))} times the unsafe wells of random order at 20% tested (not significant after correction).{envtxt} Kernel feedback found {f1(a10('M4_feedback'))} times as many ({f1(a10('M4_feedback')-a10('M1_map'))} more than the map; Holm p < 0.001), or {t_fb:.1f} tests per unsafe well against {t_rand:.1f} for random order, a {100*(1-t_fb/t_rand):.0f}% saving. Following unsafe results alone did as well ({f1(a10('H1_follow_unsafe'))}). The same held after thinning clustered wells, in spatial blocks, with California and Colorado added, and when any one US region was dropped (largest unadjusted p {'< 0.001' if LORO.wilcoxon_p.max()<0.001 else '= %.3f'%LORO.wilcoxon_p.max()}); Supporting Information (SI) gives all runs.")
if REPLAY: P(REPLAY)
P(f"Feedback works where unsafe wells cluster. Across states, enrichment rose with clustering within 20 km (Spearman ρ = {XM.loc['lift_20km','rho_vs_feedback_enrichment']:.2f}, p = {XM.loc['lift_20km','p1']:.3f}, 29 states), and in synthetic fields feedback gave no gain with a 1 km correlation length ({f1(SY.loc[1,('M4','mean')])}) and {f1(SY.loc[10,('M4','mean')])} at 10 km (Figure 3a). Feedback needs results to return quickly: with 200 wells per round, only about one update fits within a 20% budget, and enrichment fell from {f1(RB.loc[1.0,'M4_batch10'])} to {f1(RB.loc[1.0,'M4_batch200'])} (Figure 3b). ABF was more sensitive ({f1(ABB['ABF_batch200'])} at 200 wells per round).")
P(f"The answer depends on the contaminant. For fluoride above 2 mg/L (31 states), a map from other states did transfer ({f1(f2('M1_map'))}), and map-plus-feedback was best ({f1(f2('M5_hybrid'))}); for uranium (9-12 states) the pattern was mixed and low-powered (Table 1). Nitrate resembled arsenic: a map from other states reached {_n10m:.2f} and feedback {_n10f:.2f} above 10 mg/L as N (24 states; SI). A rule that fixes the weight on the map in advance therefore needs knowledge that a planner does not have. ABF removes that need by learning the weight online. Across six outcomes, ABF was never significantly worse than feedback, the hybrid or the follow-unsafe rule after correction, exceeded the worse of feedback and the hybrid in every outcome, and had a mean gap to the best fixed rule of {REG.loc['mean','ABF']:.2f} (hybrid, tuned per contaminant: {REG.loc['mean','M5_hybrid']:.2f}; feedback: {REG.loc['mean','M4_feedback']:.2f}). Its worst-case gap was {REG.loc['worst_case','ABF']:.2f}, in {NAMES[WORST]}, where the map-based experts misled it and it fell {A('as10','M4_feedback')-A('as10','ABF'):.2f} below feedback (Holm p = {S10.holm_p:.3f}). ABF is therefore a safe default and not a uniformly better rule.")
