P(f"A state's own history changes the picture. When each state's earlier wells were available (24-29 states), ABF seeded with that history found {f1(wa.loc['W2_map_with_history','mean_A'])} times random order for arsenic above 10 µg/L, against {f1(wa.loc['W2_map_with_history','mean_B'])} for a map trained with the history and {f1(wa.loc['W5_cold_feedback','mean_B'])} for cold feedback (Holm p {pfm(wa.loc['W2_map_with_history','holm_p'])} and {pfm(wa.loc['W5_cold_feedback','holm_p'])}); for fluoride the figures were {f1(wf.loc['W2_map_with_history','mean_A'])} against {f1(wf.loc['W2_map_with_history','mean_B'])} and {f1(wf.loc['W5_cold_feedback','mean_B'])} (Figure 3c). Local data are worth at least as much as data from other states, and the best plan combines history with feedback.")
fig("figures/letter_map_as10.png","Figure 1. Arsenic above 10 µg/L: unsafe wells found when 20% of each held-out state's wells are tested, relative to random order (1), for (a) a risk map built from other states and (b) adaptive testing in which each result steers the next test. Grey dots are all wells; red states beat random order, blue states fall below it.",16)
fig("figures/letter_fig1.png","Figure 2. Unsafe wells found when 20% of a new state's wells are tested, relative to random order (dashed line), for (a) arsenic and (b) fluoride. Bars are means over held-out states with 95% bootstrap intervals.")
fig("figures/letter_fig2.png","Figure 3. Why and when feedback works. (a) Synthetic fields: gain against the correlation length of unsafe wells. (b) Effect of testing in larger rounds (late results), arsenic. (c) Warm start for arsenic: each state's earlier wells available.")
P(f"Adaptive testing does not just pile up results in one hot spot. At 20% tested, ABF reached {f1(CV10['ABF'])} times the number of distinct unsafe 5-km areas found by random order for arsenic ({100*CVa10:.0f}% of all such areas against {100*CVr10:.0f}%) and {f1(CVf['ABF'])} times for fluoride ({100*CVaf:.0f}% against {100*CVrf:.0f}%), so it also finds more separate problem areas.")
P(f"Testing promotion already reaches wealthier households first ({C('Who benefits the most')}), and fairness-aware sampling is studied in machine learning ({C('Equitable Training Datasets')}). Does chasing hot spots leave poorer communities behind? We joined county poverty and income estimates ({C('Small Area Income and Poverty')}) to the arsenic wells and asked (pre-registered) whether the unsafe wells found were shared fairly between high-poverty counties (above the state median) and the rest. Adaptive testing helped poorer counties in absolute terms: it found {f1(_e('high_pov','F1','enr_low'))} times the unsafe wells there that random order would, against {f1(_e('high_pov','M1','enr_low'))} for the map. But the follow-unsafe rule under-represented them: their share of the unsafe wells found was {f1(_e('high_pov','H1','rep_ratio_mean'))} of their share of all unsafe wells (p = {_e('high_pov','H1','p_rep_vs_1'):.3f}). Splitting each batch between high-poverty and other counties in proportion to the wells still untested removed this gap (ratio {f1(_e('high_pov','H1EQ','rep_ratio_mean'))}) at almost no cost ({f1(_e('high_pov','H1','enr_all'))} to {f1(_e('high_pov','H1EQ','enr_all'))} overall) and raised the unsafe wells found in high-poverty counties to {f1(_e('high_pov','H1EQ','enr_low'))} times random order; low-income counties gave the same picture (SI).")
H1("Environmental Implications")
P(f"A risk map from other states is a weak guide for arsenic, uncertain for uranium and more useful for fluoride. A simple guide follows: (1) test whether a map transfers on held-out states before trusting it; (2) if you cannot, start with ABF or the follow-unsafe rule; (3) seed the rule with your own earlier results; and (4) return results in small batches (up to about 50 wells per round); and (5) split each batch across poorer and other areas so that hot-spot chasing does not leave poorer communities behind. Tests per unsafe well is the unit that matters for budgets: here it fell from {t_rand:.1f} to {t_fb:.1f} for arsenic.")
P(f"Limits. The wells are USGS study wells, not a random sample of private wells, so clustering may exceed that in a real campaign; thinning reduced but did not remove this concern. We simulate campaigns on wells that already have results and did not run a field trial. Three exploratory ideas did not hold up. Using every constituent measured in the same sample did not speed the search: a redox signature (nearby nitrate lowering arsenic risk) transferred between states, but a nitrate gain was not confirmed on independent wells. A forecast of unsafe wells still untested had unreliable intervals, so we offer no stopping rule; and weighting training wells by distance to the target state lifted the arsenic map above 10 µg/L but not above 5 µg/L (SI). Kansas is missing for fluoride, uranium has few states, nitrate replay was not significant, and maps lack hydrochemistry, which a planner does not hold before testing.")
