paras=["[Date of submission]","Editor-in-Chief, Environmental Science & Technology Letters","","Dear Editor,","",
f"We submit the Letter “{TITLE}” for consideration.",
f"Why it matters for environmental health. About 40 million people in the United States use private wells that fall outside public-supply regulation. Agencies and owners must decide which wells to test with limited budgets, and national machine-learning risk maps are the usual guide. We show, on 53,294 arsenic, 28,117 uranium, 150,299 fluoride and 47,375 nitrate wells held out one state at a time, how much such maps are worth in a state that supplied no data, and how much is gained by letting each test result steer the next test.",
f"What is new. (1) A budgeted, held-out-state campaign evaluation, scored in tests per unsafe well found, that shows random-split validation overstates map skill (arsenic ROC-AUC {f1(rv.loc['random_5fold','auc_roc'])} against {f1(rv.loc['leave_state_out','auc_roc'])}). (2) A finding that depends on the contaminant: for arsenic and uranium a map from other states is close to random order in a new state, even with geology, climate and elevation, whereas for fluoride it transfers. (3) An adaptive Bayesian-mixture rule (ABF) that learns online how far to trust the map, has no tuned parameter, and carries a worst-case bound on prediction loss; it was never significantly worse than the alternatives. (4) A backtest, for groundwater wells, against the order in which real USGS campaigns tested their wells (a comparable backtest exists for lead pipes in Flint), in which the order actually used found no more unsafe wells than random order while adaptive reordering found about 1.4 to 1.6 times as many for arsenic. (5) An environmental-justice check: pure hot-spot chasing under-served high-poverty counties, and batches split by county poverty removed this at almost no cost (pre-registered). (6) Measured conditions under which adaptive testing fails (large, late batches; weak spatial clustering), supported by a controlled simulation, and guidance for agencies that hold their own earlier results.",
f"Headline result. Testing next to wells that just returned unsafe results cut tests per unsafe arsenic well from {t_rand:.1f} to {t_fb:.1f} ({100*(1-t_fb/t_rand):.0f}% fewer), and it also found more distinct unsafe areas than random order.",
f"Relation to prior work. Following hits into their neighbourhood is a classical idea (adaptive cluster sampling; {C('Adaptive Cluster Sampling')}), and the closest earlier work, ActiveRemediation ({C('ActiveRemediation')}), combines a predictive model with sequential active learning for lead pipes in Flint and backtests it against the order actually used there. Transfer of groundwater models to unsampled regions has been studied for fluoride ({C('Transferability of Machine Learning Models')}; {C('National-scale evaluation of groundwater fluoride')}). We do not claim that feedback, or a backtest against real operations, is new. Our contribution is the measurement, on private-well data held out by state, of what a transferred map, feedback and a state's own history are each worth in tests; the finding that the answer depends on the contaminant; and ABF, a rule that learns the weight on the map online. In the sources we searched (PubMed, arXiv, Crossref and web search) we found no study that does this.",
"Fit with ES&T Letters. The study concerns the monitoring and protection of drinking-water sources, uses public national data and addresses a decision that agencies face now. This journal has published data-driven decision support for private wells after Hurricane Helene ({C('Responding after Hurricane Helene')}) and work on fluoride in drinking-water wells ({C('Glyphosate and Fluoride')}). The study builds on work published in ES&T on private-well arsenic maps (Lombard et al., 2021), glacial-aquifer arsenic (Erickson et al., 2021) and the transferability of groundwater models (Cao et al., 2022; Cao et al., 2024). A Supporting Information file holds the full methods, all runs and the protocol with every deviation.",
f"Format. The main text is {W['words']:,} words with {W['figures_tables']} figures/tables, with a synopsis, a TOC graphic and Supporting Information.",
"Honest limits. The wells are USGS study wells, not a random sample of private wells; we simulate campaigns on existing results and did not run a field trial; and uranium has only 9-12 test states and its replay was not significant. We state these in the Letter.",
"Originality and ethics. The work is original and not under consideration elsewhere. It uses public data and no human participants. Use of an AI assistant is declared in the manuscript. The code is available from the corresponding author on request. The authors declare no competing financial interest and received no specific funding.",
"","Sincerely,","",SECOND+" (corresponding author), on behalf of "+FIRST+" and "+SECOND,AFF,"[email address]"]
